    {
      "id": 17,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "How BatchBook logging works",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 9,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 61
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**Two different kinds of failure, logged differently.**\n\n| What happened | Severity | How to find it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Handled error — 401 bad OTP, 404 missing record, 422 bad body | `INFO`, message `request completed` | filter on `extra_status_code` |\n| Unhandled exception — a bug escaped the route handler | `ERROR`, message `request failed`, has `exception` field | filter on `severity_text=\"ERROR\"` |\n\nThis is why the panels above key on **status code**, not severity: filtering only on `ERROR` would miss every 4xx and every handled 500.\n\n**Two noise filters are applied.** Without them this dashboard is unreadable — raw log volume is over 99% noise.\n\n1. `| extra_path != \"/docs\"` — drops Render's uptime probe, which hits every 5 seconds.\n2. `| extra_path =~ \"^/(admin|attendance|batch|enrollment|fee|owner|parent|public|scores|student|teacher|webhooks)($|/.*)\"` — keeps only real BatchBook routers. Without this, bot scanners hunting `/.env`, `/graphql` and `/_ignition/execute-solution` account for the overwhelming majority of all 4xx. They are not hidden — they get their own panel above.\n\n**If you add a new router prefix in `app.py`, add it to that regex** or its failures will be misfiled as scanner noise. Current prefixes come from `routes/*.py`.\n\n**Fields available on each line** (loguru `.bind()` in `app.py:log_and_handle_exceptions`, shipped as OTel structured metadata): `extra_method`, `extra_path`, `extra_url`, `extra_status_code`, `extra_duration_ms`, `extra_query_params`, `extra_path_params`, `extra_request_body`, `extra_response_body`, `extra_trace_id`, `extra_span_id`.\n\nBodies are redacted by `request_logging.py` — tokens, passwords, OTPs and API secrets become `[REDACTED]`, and anything over 4 KB is truncated. Bodies in these panels are safe to read and safe to screenshot."
      }
    }
  ]
